# docker-compose.yml reads these — copy to .env and fill in real values before running.
# Every `change-me` value below is a placeholder the server refuses to start with.

# Spliced into DATABASE_URL as is: only URL-safe characters. `openssl rand -hex 24`
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me

# Signs sessions and Docker registry tokens. At least 32 bytes of random data:
# `openssl rand -base64 48`. Shorter values are refused at startup.
JWT_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# Encrypts secrets at rest (SMTP/LDAP/OIDC/proxy-repository credentials, TOTP seeds).
# Mandatory. Must be its own random value, distinct from JWT_SECRET, so that a leaked
# JWT_SECRET (which forges sessions) does not also decrypt every stored secret. At least
# 32 bytes, and random: it is expanded with HKDF, which cannot strengthen a passphrase.
# Generate one with `openssl rand -base64 48`. To change it later, see "Rotating
# SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY" in the README.
SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=change-me-to-a-different-long-random-string

# Only while rotating SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY: the key the stored secrets are encrypted with today.
# SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# `true` lets the startup pass rewrite stored secrets to the current format, or to the new key after a rotation.
# Leave it unset until an upgrade is verified: releases before the versioned secret format cannot read the rewritten values.
# Rolling back the image after the first start of a release that added migrations needs a database restore either way (see "Upgrading" in the README).
# SECRETS_REENCRYPT_LEGACY=true

# The URL clients reach this deployment at, with its scheme. Mandatory: it decides the
# Docker token realm, HSTS, the passkey origin and canonical URLs, and the server refuses to
# start when it points at 0.0.0.0.
PUBLIC_URL=https://app.artiferris.example.com

# The base domain organizations are resolved as subdomains of (e.g. "artiferris.example" so
# that "acme.artiferris.example" resolves the "acme" organization). Mandatory — the server
# refuses to start without it.
ARTIFERRIS_BASE_DOMAIN=artiferris.example.com

# Optional: only creates an account when the users table is empty (safe to leave
# set across restarts). Without these, there is no way to create the first admin
# account through the running application — leave both unset only if you already
# have another way to seed the first super-admin. The password must be at least
# 8 characters — a too-short value fails silently (logged, not fatal) and leaves
# the deployment with no admin; fix the value and restart to retry.
ARTIFERRIS_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ARTIFERRIS_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# Private ranges (comma-separated CIDRs or addresses) that admin-configured LDAP, SMTP, OIDC
# and proxy-repository hosts may resolve to. Everything private is refused by default, which
# rules out an on-premises directory or mail relay; list only what you need. Unencrypted SMTP
# is only accepted for hosts listed here. A typo stops the server at startup, and so does a /0 range (it would switch the guard off).
# ARTIFERRIS_SSRF_ALLOWED_CIDRS=10.20.0.0/16

# Set this if you run behind a reverse proxy: the IPs or CIDR ranges (10.0.0.0/8, fd00::/8) that proxy
# connects from, comma-separated (list every hop if you chain them). A typo stops the server at startup. Only those peers get their X-Forwarded-For believed, and the
# client is read from its right-most entry that isn't one of these — anything further left is
# whatever the client itself sent. Left unset behind a proxy, every request looks like it comes
# from the proxy, so the per-IP login/registration throttle becomes one shared bucket for every
# client behind it: one attacker locks everyone out, and no attacker is ever isolated. The server logs a
# warning (at most once an hour) when requests carry X-Forwarded-For from a private address while this is empty.
# A /0 range (0.0.0.0/0, ::/0) is refused: it would trust the whole internet.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=10.0.0.4,10.0.0.5

# Optional: days the security and administrative audit events (logins, privilege and configuration changes) are kept before a sweep (five minutes after
# startup, then daily) deletes them. Default 365, 0 keeps everything, at most 36500. Package, repository and permission events are never deleted.
# AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=365

# Optional: restricts CORS to this single origin instead of the default (unset) policy,
# which only allows localhost/127.0.0.1/[::1] on any port — enough for a separately served
# Angular dev server (`ng serve`) against this backend, but not an arbitrary internet origin.
# Must be a bare scheme+host origin with no trailing slash and no path, e.g.:
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=https://artiferris.example.com

# Leave this unset — the default. The realm URL embedded in every
# WWW-Authenticate challenge (what `docker login`/`push`/`pull` resolves
# token requests against) is derived per request from that request's Host
# header and PUBLIC_URL's scheme, correct for any number of organization
# subdomains. Only set it if this deployment sits behind something that
# doesn't forward Host/scheme faithfully — that pins every client to one
# fixed realm instead. Must be https:// for a non-localhost host (Docker
# refuses plain http:// otherwise).
# ARTIFERRIS_DOCKER_TOKEN_REALM=https://artiferris.example.com/v2/token
